1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,466
This is a messy inbox.

2
00:00:01,466 --> 00:00:04,949
Names, order numbers, dates, phone numbers, all mixed together.

3
00:00:04,949 --> 00:00:05,502
Now watch.

4
00:00:05,502 --> 00:00:08,930
One short pattern, and Python found every email address in it.

5
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:13,131
Not by searching for one email, but by describing what any email looks like.

6
00:00:13,131 --> 00:00:15,564
That pattern is called a regular expression.

7
00:00:15,564 --> 00:00:18,605
And by the end of this video, you'll write it yourself.

8
00:00:19,305 --> 00:00:20,415
Regex sounds scary.

9
00:00:20,415 --> 00:00:23,163
It looks like a cat walked across the keyboard.

10
00:00:23,163 --> 00:00:31,756
But it's built from a handful of small pieces, and once you know them, you can find phone numbers, dates, codes, anything with a shape, in seconds.

11
00:00:31,756 --> 00:00:33,159
Step by step, from zero.

12
00:00:33,859 --> 00:00:35,071
Here's the big idea.

13
00:00:35,071 --> 00:00:39,070
Normal find, the Control F you already know, looks for exact text.

14
00:00:39,070 --> 00:00:42,039
Type five five five, and it finds five five five.

15
00:00:42,039 --> 00:00:44,403
A regular expression looks for a shape.

16
00:00:44,403 --> 00:00:47,796
Three digits, a dash, three digits, a dash, four digits.

17
00:00:47,796 --> 00:00:49,553
It doesn't care which digits.

18
00:00:49,553 --> 00:00:51,553
That's what makes it so powerful.

19
00:00:52,253 --> 00:00:53,171
Here's the plan.

20
00:00:53,171 --> 00:00:55,007
Learn the basic building blocks.

21
00:00:55,007 --> 00:00:56,384
Find every phone number.

22
00:00:56,384 --> 00:00:57,360
Find every email.

23
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:59,769
Pull dates apart into day, month and year.

24
00:00:59,769 --> 00:01:01,892
Hide sensitive numbers automatically.

25
00:01:01,892 --> 00:01:03,556
And check if a code is valid.

26
00:01:03,556 --> 00:01:06,597
Then the three mistakes everyone makes, so you don't.

27
00:01:07,298 --> 00:01:08,669
All you need is Python.

28
00:01:08,669 --> 00:01:13,976
Regex lives in a module called r e, and it comes built in, so there's nothing to install.

29
00:01:13,976 --> 00:01:17,792
Make a folder, and put a text file in it called inbox dot t x t.

30
00:01:17,792 --> 00:01:21,668
You can use any text you like, or copy mine from the description.

31
00:01:21,668 --> 00:01:24,352
Then check your Python version in a terminal.

32
00:01:25,052 --> 00:01:26,316
Create step one dot py.

33
00:01:26,316 --> 00:01:26,921
Import r e.

34
00:01:26,921 --> 00:01:29,836
Then read the whole file into a variable called text.

35
00:01:29,836 --> 00:01:31,871
Now, re dot findall takes two things.

36
00:01:31,871 --> 00:01:33,741
A pattern, and the text to search.

37
00:01:33,741 --> 00:01:35,665
It returns a list with every match.

38
00:01:35,665 --> 00:01:37,755
First pattern, the plain word Invoice.

39
00:01:37,755 --> 00:01:39,020
Then, backslash d plus.

40
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:41,110
Notice the little r before the quotes.

41
00:01:41,110 --> 00:01:43,090
That's a raw string, and it matters.

42
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:44,410
I'll show you why later.

43
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:45,530
Run it.

44
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:48,415
The plain word finds exactly one match, Invoice.

45
00:01:48,415 --> 00:01:51,539
But backslash d plus found every number in the file.

46
00:01:51,539 --> 00:01:54,544
Order numbers, years, months, phone number pieces.

47
00:01:54,544 --> 00:01:55,625
Seventeen of them.

48
00:01:55,625 --> 00:01:57,488
We never told it which numbers.

49
00:01:57,488 --> 00:01:58,750
We just said, digits.

50
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:01,248
Let's slow down and see why.

51
00:02:01,248 --> 00:02:04,909
Backslash d means one digit, any digit from zero to nine.

52
00:02:04,909 --> 00:02:08,121
And plus means one or more of the thing before it.

53
00:02:08,121 --> 00:02:11,268
So together, a run of digits, as long as it goes.

54
00:02:11,268 --> 00:02:14,030
Watch it light up every number in the text.

55
00:02:14,730 --> 00:02:18,434
Here are the building blocks you'll use ninety percent of the time.

56
00:02:18,434 --> 00:02:19,595
Backslash d, a digit.

57
00:02:19,595 --> 00:02:23,022
Backslash w, a word character, letters, digits, or underscore.

58
00:02:23,022 --> 00:02:24,183
Backslash s, a space.

59
00:02:24,183 --> 00:02:26,008
A dot means any character at all.

60
00:02:26,008 --> 00:02:30,762
Plus is one or more, star is zero or more, and question mark makes something optional.

61
00:02:30,762 --> 00:02:33,526
Curly braces with a number mean exactly that many.

62
00:02:33,526 --> 00:02:36,733
And square brackets mean any one of the characters inside.

63
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:37,964
Step two.

64
00:02:37,964 --> 00:02:38,791
Phone numbers.

65
00:02:38,791 --> 00:02:43,867
Our numbers look like five five five, dash, two oh one, dash, seven seven eight eight.

66
00:02:43,867 --> 00:02:45,579
So let's describe that shape.

67
00:02:45,579 --> 00:02:48,708
Backslash d, curly three, means exactly three digits.

68
00:02:48,708 --> 00:02:51,424
Then a dash, which is just a normal character.

69
00:02:51,424 --> 00:02:54,316
Three more digits, another dash, and four digits.

70
00:02:55,016 --> 00:02:56,826
In code, it's the same findall.

71
00:02:56,826 --> 00:03:00,680
Put the pattern in, and save the list in a variable called phones.

72
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:03,366
Then print the list, and its length, with len.

73
00:03:04,066 --> 00:03:04,461
Run it.

74
00:03:04,461 --> 00:03:06,605
Three phone numbers, and nothing else.

75
00:03:06,605 --> 00:03:08,637
Not the order number, not the dates.

76
00:03:08,637 --> 00:03:13,603
The dates have dashes too, but they have four digits first, so they don't fit the shape.

77
00:03:13,603 --> 00:03:16,086
That's the precision you get with a pattern.

78
00:03:16,786 --> 00:03:17,398
Step three.

79
00:03:17,398 --> 00:03:19,345
Emails, the pattern from the start.

80
00:03:19,345 --> 00:03:21,014
Let's build it piece by piece.

81
00:03:21,014 --> 00:03:22,962
First, the name before the at sign.

82
00:03:22,962 --> 00:03:27,803
Square brackets with backslash w, dot, plus and dash mean, any one of these characters.

83
00:03:27,803 --> 00:03:30,752
The plus after the bracket means one or more of them.

84
00:03:30,752 --> 00:03:31,698
Then the at sign.

85
00:03:31,698 --> 00:03:34,369
Then the domain name, word characters or dashes.

86
00:03:34,369 --> 00:03:36,094
Then backslash dot, a real dot.

87
00:03:36,094 --> 00:03:38,319
And finally the ending, like com or org.

88
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:41,277
Why backslash dot, and not just a dot?

89
00:03:41,277 --> 00:03:44,306
Because, remember, a plain dot means any character.

90
00:03:44,306 --> 00:03:48,346
Backslash in front turns a special character back into a normal one.

91
00:03:48,346 --> 00:03:49,712
That's called escaping.

92
00:03:49,712 --> 00:03:53,514
In the code, we loop over the list and print one email per line.

93
00:03:54,214 --> 00:03:54,639
Run it.

94
00:03:54,639 --> 00:03:55,672
All three emails.

95
00:03:55,672 --> 00:03:57,069
Notice the tricky ones.

96
00:03:57,069 --> 00:03:59,985
Tom's uses an underscore, and a dot cafe ending.

97
00:03:59,985 --> 00:04:02,111
Dana's has a plus sign in the name.

98
00:04:02,111 --> 00:04:07,214
The pattern handles both, because we listed those characters in the square brackets.

99
00:04:07,914 --> 00:04:08,830
Step four, groups.

100
00:04:08,830 --> 00:04:10,815
Our dates are written year, month, day.

101
00:04:10,815 --> 00:04:13,258
But say you want day, slash, month, slash, year.

102
00:04:13,258 --> 00:04:15,853
Put round brackets around each part of the pattern.

103
00:04:15,853 --> 00:04:17,533
Each pair of brackets is a group.

104
00:04:17,533 --> 00:04:21,095
Python finds the whole date, and also remembers each group separately.

105
00:04:21,095 --> 00:04:23,182
Watch the colored lines under each match.

106
00:04:23,182 --> 00:04:26,184
Blue for the year, yellow for the month, green for the day.

107
00:04:26,884 --> 00:04:31,458
When a pattern has groups, findall gives you the groups instead of the whole match.

108
00:04:31,458 --> 00:04:34,269
One small tuple per date, with year, month and day.

109
00:04:34,269 --> 00:04:38,733
So we can unpack them right in the for loop, and print them in any order we like.

110
00:04:39,433 --> 00:04:39,855
Run it.

111
00:04:39,855 --> 00:04:42,506
Third of October, and twentieth of November.

112
00:04:42,506 --> 00:04:43,892
Same dates, new format.

113
00:04:43,892 --> 00:04:48,231
This is how you clean up dates from a hundred different files in one go.

114
00:04:48,931 --> 00:04:49,535
Step five.

115
00:04:49,535 --> 00:04:52,135
Finding is great, but you can also replace.

116
00:04:52,135 --> 00:04:55,822
Say you want to share this inbox, but hide the phone numbers.

117
00:04:55,822 --> 00:04:59,510
re dot sub finds every match and swaps it for something else.

118
00:04:59,510 --> 00:04:59,872
Watch.

119
00:04:59,872 --> 00:05:03,076
Every phone number lights up, and then gets replaced.

120
00:05:03,076 --> 00:05:05,131
Only the last four digits survive.

121
00:05:05,832 --> 00:05:06,888
Here's the trick.

122
00:05:06,888 --> 00:05:11,423
We put round brackets around the last four digits, making them group one.

123
00:05:11,423 --> 00:05:15,462
In the replacement, backslash one means, put group one back here.

124
00:05:15,462 --> 00:05:19,004
So XXX dash XXX dash, and then the real last four digits.

125
00:05:19,004 --> 00:05:23,291
Both strings start with r, raw strings, because both use backslashes.

126
00:05:23,991 --> 00:05:24,456
Run it.

127
00:05:24,456 --> 00:05:27,778
The whole inbox, and every phone number is masked.

128
00:05:27,778 --> 00:05:31,099
The emails, dates and order numbers are untouched.

129
00:05:31,099 --> 00:05:35,285
Imagine doing this by hand across a thousand customer messages.

130
00:05:35,985 --> 00:05:36,509
Step six.

131
00:05:36,509 --> 00:05:37,382
Checking input.

132
00:05:37,382 --> 00:05:41,514
Say product codes must be two capital letters, a dash, and four digits.

133
00:05:41,514 --> 00:05:46,460
Square brackets, A dash Z, means any capital letter, and curly two means two of them.

134
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:48,090
The key is re dot fullmatch.

135
00:05:48,090 --> 00:05:53,444
It only says yes if the whole string fits the pattern, from the first character to the last.

136
00:05:54,144 --> 00:05:54,547
Run it.

137
00:05:54,547 --> 00:05:56,675
A B dash one two three four is valid.

138
00:05:56,675 --> 00:05:59,780
Lowercase fails, because we asked for capital letters.

139
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:00,816
Five digits fails.

140
00:06:00,816 --> 00:06:03,058
And an extra letter in front fails too.

141
00:06:03,058 --> 00:06:10,708
With re dot search instead, those last two would sneak through, because search is happy to find the pattern anywhere inside the text.

142
00:06:11,408 --> 00:06:13,714
Now, the three mistakes everyone makes.

143
00:06:13,714 --> 00:06:14,424
Mistake one.

144
00:06:14,424 --> 00:06:16,849
You want each word inside angle brackets.

145
00:06:16,849 --> 00:06:19,689
So you write, less than, dot star, greater than.

146
00:06:19,689 --> 00:06:24,776
But it grabs everything from the first bracket to the very last one, as one big match.

147
00:06:24,776 --> 00:06:25,900
Dot star is greedy.

148
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:27,438
It eats as much as it can.

149
00:06:28,138 --> 00:06:30,024
The fix is one question mark.

150
00:06:30,024 --> 00:06:32,042
Dot star question mark is lazy.

151
00:06:32,042 --> 00:06:34,970
It stops at the first closing bracket it can.

152
00:06:34,970 --> 00:06:37,768
Now we get two clean matches, red and blue.

153
00:06:38,468 --> 00:06:39,164
Mistake two.

154
00:06:39,164 --> 00:06:40,150
Forgetting the r.

155
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:43,282
Backslash b means a word boundary, the edge of a word.

156
00:06:43,282 --> 00:06:49,141
Without the r, Python turns backslash b into a special invisible character before regex ever sees it.

157
00:06:49,141 --> 00:06:51,404
So you get an empty list, and no error.

158
00:06:51,404 --> 00:06:52,854
With the r, it finds cat.

159
00:06:52,854 --> 00:06:54,246
Just always write the r.

160
00:06:54,246 --> 00:06:55,116
It never hurts.

161
00:06:55,816 --> 00:06:56,619
Mistake three.

162
00:06:56,619 --> 00:06:57,652
The unescaped dot.

163
00:06:57,652 --> 00:07:04,021
A dot in your pattern matches any character, so this date pattern also matched two oh two six x ten x oh three.

164
00:07:04,021 --> 00:07:07,005
Escape it with a backslash when you mean a real dot.

165
00:07:07,005 --> 00:07:13,259
And if you ever see re dot error, missing closing bracket, you opened a round bracket and forgot to close it.

166
00:07:13,259 --> 00:07:14,406
Count your brackets.

167
00:07:15,106 --> 00:07:15,618
Pro tip.

168
00:07:15,618 --> 00:07:17,152
Regex is case sensitive.

169
00:07:17,152 --> 00:07:23,611
Search for invoice with a small i, and you get nothing, because the text says Invoice with a capital.

170
00:07:23,611 --> 00:07:29,366
Add re dot IGNORECASE as a third argument, and it matches any mix of upper and lower case.

171
00:07:30,066 --> 00:07:30,877
And one more.

172
00:07:30,877 --> 00:07:35,369
Most code editors, and many text editors, let you search with regex too.

173
00:07:35,369 --> 00:07:38,177
Look for a dot star button in the search box.

174
00:07:38,177 --> 00:07:42,607
Turn it on, and every pattern from today works there, no Python needed.

175
00:07:42,607 --> 00:07:44,666
It's the fastest way to practice.

176
00:07:45,366 --> 00:07:46,126
Quick recap.

177
00:07:46,126 --> 00:07:46,823
Import r e.

178
00:07:46,823 --> 00:07:48,787
Use findall to get every match.

179
00:07:48,787 --> 00:07:56,072
Backslash d for digits, backslash w for word characters, plus for one or more, and curly braces for an exact count.

180
00:07:56,072 --> 00:07:57,782
Round brackets make groups.

181
00:07:57,782 --> 00:08:01,393
re dot sub replaces, and fullmatch checks a whole string.

182
00:08:01,393 --> 00:08:03,103
And always use raw strings.

183
00:08:03,803 --> 00:08:10,408
Next on Shortcut Lab, make a Python script run automatically, every single day, without you touching it.

184
00:08:10,408 --> 00:08:11,806
On Windows and on Mac.

185
00:08:11,806 --> 00:08:13,838
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
